\begin{abstract}
The gauge sector of quantum spin ice realizes compact $U(1)$
electrodynamics, but its hallmark excitation --- the emergent photon ---
is described, in every analytic prediction of the neutron-accessible
structure factor, at the Gaussian level, where the electric field
creates exactly one photon and $\Szz(\bq,\omega)$ is exhausted by a
single doubly degenerate pole; no calculation has resolved how the
compact theory actually distributes this weight.  We resolve it
exactly: the ring-exchange model is diagonalized in the full ice sector
of 48- and 64-site pyrochlore tori (the standard 16- and 32-site cells
are shown to be unusable, their ring dynamics being accidentally
trivial), every dominant level is classified by an exact
polarization-resolved decomposition of the probe and by its
Hellmann--Feynman response to a Rokhsar--Kivelson deformation, and the
result is compared with the Gaussian theory quantized on the same
lattice, whose two transverse polarizations are exactly degenerate at
every accessible momentum.  Three results follow.  (i)~Both transverse
photon branches survive at every momentum, with interaction splittings
ranging from below $1\%$ to $O(1)$.  (ii)~At the zone-boundary $L$
point the second branch \emph{rotonizes}: it drops a factor of two
below the Gaussian line, carries half of the neutron spectral weight,
and is the lowest excitation of the theory.  The mechanism is
Feynman--Bijl: the equal-time structure factor of the ice liquid is
maximal at $L$, and momentum classes with larger $S(\bq)$ rotonize
while those with smaller $S(\bq)$ do not.  The roton is
resonance-rich, softens toward the flippability-rich side of the phase
diagram, and is stable from 48 to 64 sites ($0.51\to0.50$ of the
weight).  (iii)~Beyond the two branches, $13$--$38\%$ of $\Szz$ is
multiparticle weight, confined by an exact charge-conjugation parity
to the $C$-odd sector and falling to $13\%$ at the smallest accessible
momentum, consistent with infrared protection; the $C$-even sector is
exactly dark in $\Szz$ and is instead the leading response of
bond-bilinear probes such as Raman scattering and ultrasound.  The
emergent photon of quantum spin ice thus carries a helium-like roton
branch and a large multiparticle continuum --- both invisible to the
Gaussian description used to analyze current experiments.
\end{abstract}

\maketitle

\emph{Introduction.}---Quantum spin ice (QSI) is the canonical
three-dimensional $U(1)$ quantum spin liquid: virtual exchange on the
two-in--two-out ice manifold of the pyrochlore lattice generates a
six-spin ring exchange, and the resulting low-energy theory is a compact
$U(1)$ lattice gauge theory with gapped electric charges and an emergent
photon~\cite{Hermele2004,Gingras2014}.  The photon's experimental
fingerprint is carried by the dynamical structure factor: the local
moment maps to the emergent electric field, $S^z=E$, so
$\Szz(\bq,\omega)$ measures electric-field fluctuations, and suppressed
low-energy weight together with a linearly dispersing mode is the
signature sought by neutron scattering
~\cite{Benton2012,Sibille2018,Gao2025}.

Every \emph{analytic} prediction for this observable is Gaussian.  The compact
cosine of the gauge field is expanded to quadratic order, the electric
field becomes linear in photon operators, and \emph{all} of the
$\Szz$ sum rule sits on the one-photon pole
~\cite{Hermele2004,Benton2012,Desrochers2023}.  The corrections are not
obviously small: the ring-exchange coupling is of lattice scale, the
emergent fine-structure constant is of order
$0.1$~\cite{Pace2021}, semiclassical treatments find $O(1)$ anharmonic
renormalizations of the photon bandwidth
~\cite{Kwasigroch2017,Kwasigroch2020}, and quantum Monte Carlo reports a
zone-boundary photon linewidth comparable to the entire photon bandwidth,
explicitly ``not captured'' by the Gaussian theory~\cite{Huang2018}.  The
one analysis of photon--photon interactions argued that the decay of one
photon into three vanishes kinematically~\cite{Kwasigroch2017}, but the
argument applies only to the linear part of the dispersion; at the zone
boundary, where the photon band is flat and where the finite-momentum
experiments and simulations actually operate, it does not apply, and the
multi-photon content of $\Szz$ has never been computed.

Multi-photon states of the compact photon are themselves long
established: in four-dimensional compact QED, photon-pair and
three-photon scattering states and photon-channel resonances were
identified from finite-volume energy levels and avoided crossings three
decades ago~\cite{Cox1997}, and the dynamical dimer correlations at the
Rokhsar--Kivelson point of the cubic-lattice dimer model show the
remnant of the Coulomb-phase photon~\cite{Lauchli2008}.  What has not
been computed, there or in quantum spin ice, is the
spectral-\emph{weight} decomposition of a gauge-invariant field
correlator in the deconfined phase: which fraction of the measured
response is one photon, and where the remainder sits.

Here we compute it exactly, at the level of the compact gauge theory
itself, and compare with the Gaussian theory quantized on the same
lattice, so that every deviation is unambiguously an effect of
compactness.

\emph{Model.}---We study the minimal compact theory: the ring-exchange
Hamiltonian on the ice manifold,
\begin{equation}
 H=-K\sum_{p}\left(W_{p}+W_{p}^{\dagger}\right),
 \qquad W_{p}=S_1^+S_2^-S_3^+S_4^-S_5^+S_6^-,
 \label{eq:ring}
\end{equation}
with the sum over the contractible hexagonal plaquettes of a periodic
pyrochlore cluster and $K>0$ (the zero-flux sector).  In gauge language
$W_{p}=e^{iB_{p}}$, so Eq.~(\ref{eq:ring}) is
$-2K\sum\cos B$: the full compact interaction, with no expansion.
Equation~(\ref{eq:ring}) conserves the electric-flux (transport) sectors
of the torus exactly, and contains neither spinons --- the Hilbert space
is the ice manifold --- nor the noncontractible ``winding'' plaquettes
that generate torus artifacts in microscopic finite-cluster
calculations~\cite{ZhouKimMask}: on our cluster $100$ of the $148$
six-cycles wrap the periodic box and are excluded by construction.
The Hilbert space contains no spinons; it does, however, contain the
full magnetic (flux) sector of the gauge theory, so structure beyond
the one-photon pole may in principle comprise both multi-photon and
magnetic (monopole-pair) contributions~\cite{Kwasigroch2020} ---
separating the two is part of the analysis below.

\emph{Cluster selection.}---A finite-size subtlety, to our knowledge
unreported, makes the standard clusters unusable here.  On the 32-site
fcc cell ($2{\times}2{\times}2$ primitive cells, 2970 ice states) the
connected part of the zero-flux sector is an exactly 8-regular graph:
every one of its 384 resonating configurations has exactly eight
flippable hexagons~\cite{Hermele2004}.  The equal-amplitude state is then
an exact eigenstate, a Rokhsar--Kivelson potential $V\sum_{p}
n^{\rm flip}_{p}$ is a constant on the sector, the spectrum is
integer, and the ``photon'' does not disperse: $\omega_X/\omega_L=2$
exactly, independent of any coupling.  On the 16-site cubic cell, direct
diagonalization shows the ring dynamics to be trivial: only six of the
25 transport sectors possess any dynamics, each an 8-state block with
ground energy $-4K$ exactly, the ground state is six-fold
sector-degenerate, and the largest sector contains no flippable
hexagons at all.  Neither
cluster supports emergent photon dynamics in the compact theory, a
pathology invisible to calculations that never resolve the gauge sector
and directly relevant to the interpretation of small-cluster
diagonalizations.  [Large-scale projected diagonalizations have
implicitly encountered related shape effects~\cite{Pace2021}.]

We therefore use the $2{\times}2{\times}3$ fcc cluster: 48 sites,
$87\,546$ ice states, 48 contractible hexagons, and 12 momenta.  Its
flippability distribution is broad (6--14 flippable hexagons across the
ground sector), and the exact ground energy, $E_0=-10.556K$, differs from
the equal-amplitude bound set by the mean flippability ($10.039$),
confirming genuinely correlated ring dynamics.  The ground state is
unique and lies entirely in a single transport sector of dimension
$9104$, which we diagonalize completely; $\Szz$ is then evaluated
level-by-level with no continued-fraction broadening.  For the size test we additionally treat the $2{\times}2{\times}4$
cluster: 64 sites, $2\,756\,394$ ice states, ground sector of
dimension $249\,180$, solved by Lanczos continued fraction after the
pipeline reproduced every exact 48-site benchmark (ground energy to
$10^{-9}$; the $L$-point lines and weights to $10^{-3}$).  Momentum
assignments are certified by exact translation projectors:
$\sum_{\bq}\lVert P_{\bq}v\rVert^2=1$ to $10^{-10}$ for every
eigenvector, and the probe identity
$\lVert P_{\bq}\,S^z(\bq)\vert0\rangle\rVert^2
 =\lVert S^z(\bq)\vert0\rangle\rVert^2$ holds to $10^{-8}$.  Because
the complete sector eigenbasis is used and $S^z(\bq)\vert0\rangle$ lies
entirely within the sector, the level-resolved weights saturate the
equal-time correlator
$\langle0\vert S^z(-\bq)S^z(\bq)\vert0\rangle$ identically: no
spectral weight lies outside the computed set.

\emph{Gaussian theory on the same lattice.}---The comparison theory is
$H_G=\frac{U}{2}\sum_\ell E_\ell^2+K\sum_{p}B_{p}^2$
quantized on the identical 48-site torus, with $B=CA$ and $C$ the
alternating lattice curl over the same 48 contractible hexagons.  $C$
annihilates every lattice gradient exactly and has rank
$22=48-23-3$, the correct topological count (gradients plus three
harmonic forms).  Its singular values give the photon branches,
$\omega_\lambda(\bq)=\sqrt{2UK}\,\sigma_\lambda(\bq)$, and take closed
forms on the momentum set
($\sigma=3,\,2\sqrt3,\,\sqrt{13},\,\sqrt{15},\,4$).  In the pure ring
model the electric stiffness $U$ is generated rather than bare
~\cite{Hermele2004,Benton2012,Kwasigroch2017}, so one overall scale is
fitted --- to the Rokhsar--Kivelson-classified photon lines only
(Table~\ref{tab:main}), excluding the rotonized branch-2 level at $L$, giving
$\sqrt{2UK}=0.587$; every ratio between momenta and branches, and the
entire weight distribution, is parameter-free lattice geometry.  Two
Gaussian statements matter below.  First, since $E$ is linear in photon
operators, $H_G$ places the \emph{entire} $\Szz$ sum rule on the
one-photon lines.  Second, we find the two transverse branches to be
\emph{exactly degenerate at every allowed momentum} of this cluster:
the Gaussian theory predicts a single doubly-degenerate line per
momentum, even on this anisotropic torus.

An exact selection rule sharpens the comparison.  The global spin flip
$\mathcal P:S^z\!\to\!-S^z$, $S^\pm\!\to\!S^\mp$ commutes with
Eq.~(\ref{eq:ring}) and, in gauge language, is the charge conjugation
$E\to-E$, $B\to-B$: an $n$-photon state carries parity $(-1)^n$.  The exact statement is
that $S^z$ is $C$-odd, so $\Szz$ couples only to the $C=-1$ sector;
odd photon number is the photon-basis reading of that sector, and once
magnetic excitations are admitted, $C$ --- not photon number --- is the
conserved label.  The $C$-even sector, in particular the two-photon
continuum, is exactly dark in neutron response and appears instead in
$C$-even bond-bilinear channels --- Raman scattering,
where the two-photon density of states has been computed at Gaussian
order~\cite{Fu2017}, and ultrasound~\cite{Simon2022}.

