\emph{Results.}---Table~\ref{tab:main} and Fig.~\ref{fig:main} contain
the comparison; every statement below is classified by two independent
diagnostics.  The \emph{polarization decomposition} projects the probe
onto the two Gaussian transverse directions (an exact operation: the
four singular directions resolve the identity on sublattice space to
$10^{-17}$, and the longitudinal residue is $\lesssim10^{-32}$,
verifying $\nabla\!\cdot\!E=0$).  The \emph{Rokhsar--Kivelson response}
tracks every dominant level through $H\to H+V\sum_p n^{\rm flip}_p$;
by Hellmann--Feynman the slope $d(\Delta E)/dV$ measures the
excitation's flippability relative to the ground state and serves as a
character diagnostic.

\emph{(i) Both photon branches survive.}  At every momentum the two
dominant levels couple to the two transverse polarizations: exactly
orthogonally at $X$ (the $3.42K$ level is purely the second branch),
and with complementary majorities at $L$ ($68\%/32\%$ against
$27\%/73\%$: the two branches hybridize by $\sim30\%$ there).  Because
the Gaussian degeneracy is exact, every splitting is a photon--photon
interaction effect --- the effect anticipated semiclassically in
Ref.~\cite{Kwasigroch2017}, here measured: $4.5\%$ at
$(\tfrac13\tfrac13\tfrac13)$, $19\%$ at $(\tfrac13\tfrac13\tfrac23)$,
$6\%$ at $(\tfrac16\tfrac16\tfrac56)$, $0.2\%$ at the 64-site momentum
$(\tfrac14\tfrac14\tfrac34)$, and $O(1)$ at the zone boundary.  [The
$53\%$ splitting at $X$ on the 48-site torus shrinks to $6\%$ at 64
sites; $X$ is not size-converged and we do not build on it.]

\emph{(ii) The second branch rotonizes at $L$.}  At $L$ the
branch-resolved spectrum is: branch~1 at $1.730K$ ($32\%$ of the
weight, RK slope $-0.05$, $15\%$ below the Gaussian line --- an
ordinary dressed photon), and branch~2 at $1.024K$, a factor of two
below the Gaussian line, carrying $51\%$ of the neutron weight, with
RK slope $+0.34$: interactions have transformed its character to
\emph{resonance-rich} (more flippable hexagons than the ground state).
The slope is a character diagnostic, not a photon-number one --- the
polarization decomposition shows this level to be the second
transverse branch, not an additional particle.  Two- and multi-photon
character are excluded by parity and kinematics (the cheapest
three-photon combination of exact lines costs $\gtrsim5.2K$).

The mechanism is Feynman--Bijl.  The equal-time structure factor is
maximal at $L$ [$S(\bq)=0.370$ against $0.19$--$0.29$ elsewhere], and
the exact first-moment bound $\omega_{\rm SMA}=f_1(\bq)/S(\bq)$ has its
minimum there ($1.85K$ against $2.4$--$3.2K$): where the ice liquid's
correlations peak, a collective density mode drops below the photon
line, exactly as the roton drops below the phonon in superfluid
helium.  The 64-site torus makes this predictive: its $L$-type momenta
split into two inequivalent classes, and the class with the larger
$S(\bq)$ rotonizes ($0.955K$, $50.0\%$ of the weight) while the class
with the smaller $S(\bq)$ does not (photon-dominant at $1.998K$) ---
rotonization tracks the structure-factor peak class by class within a
single cluster.  Under a Rokhsar--Kivelson deformation the roton
softens monotonically ($1.02K\to0.73K$ by $V/K=-1.1$) with $S_L$
growing, identifying it as the precursor mode of the
flippability-rich side of the phase diagram, though no condensation or
sector crossing occurs in the scanned range; at negative $V$ the
star-degenerate rotons are the lowest excitations of the entire model.

\emph{(iii) The multiparticle weight.}  Beyond the two branches,
$16$--$38\%$ of the $\Szz$ weight on the 48-site torus
($13$--$34\%$ at 64 sites) lies in higher levels --- against exactly
zero in the Gaussian theory at any parameter values.  By the parity
rule it is confined to the $C$-odd sector; its candidates are
multiparticle photon/roton states and magnetic (monopole-pair)
excitations --- the ice manifold removes spinons but retains the full
magnetic sector~\cite{Kwasigroch2020}.  The RK slope of the largest
such group at $(\tfrac13\tfrac13\tfrac13)$, $-0.57$, is of the
magnitude of sums of branch slopes, consistent with composite
character.  At the smallest accessible momentum
[$(\tfrac14\tfrac14\tfrac14)$, 64 sites] the beyond-branch weight
falls to $13\%$ and the dominant line carries $85\%$ --- the trend
required by infrared protection, under which the Gaussian description
is recovered as $\bq\to0$.  We distinguish throughout the finite-torus
statement (discrete levels carrying this weight, stable from 48 to 64
sites) from its thermodynamic-limit interpretation as a continuum.
