\emph{(iv) The even channel.}---The $C$-even states invisible to
$\Szz$ are the leading response of the plaquette correlator
$\Phi(\bq)=\sum_p e^{-i\bq\cdot\bm r_p}(W_p+W_p^\dagger)$, the lattice
analogue of the Raman and ultrasound
vertices~\cite{Fu2017,Simon2022}.  Figure~\ref{fig:channels} shows
both channels: every level is bright in one panel only.  The
even-channel onset at $X$, $\omega=2.23K$, numerically suggests two
roton quanta ($2\times1.02K$; $L+L=X$), but the measured RK slope of
the edge state, $+0.30$, refutes a weakly bound pair ($+0.68$
predicted) as well as a photon pair ($-0.1$); it instead matches the
roton itself ($+0.34$): the edge is a single $C$-even state of the
roton family --- the even-parity face of the same zone-boundary
physics.  A kinematic point relevant to experiment follows from
gaplessness: in the thermodynamic limit the multi-quantum continua
extend down to the one-photon line itself, so in both channels the
line is distinguished from its continuum by spectral weight rather
than by any gap --- lineshape analysis, not peak counting, is the
appropriate strategy.  (On the finite torus the smallest nonzero
photon energy gaps these thresholds, which is why the region below
$\sim2.2K$ in Fig.~\ref{fig:channels}(c) is empty.)

\emph{Lattice versus continuum.}---These are statements about the lattice
gauge theory, not its infrared limit, and the distinction is physical.
At small momenta the Gaussian description is protected: the quartic
vertex is irrelevant and on-shell $1\to3$ decay is kinematically
forbidden on the linear dispersion~\cite{Kwasigroch2017}, so
$f_{\rm res}$ must vanish as $\bq\to0$ and continuum electrodynamics is
recovered.  At the zone boundary there is no scale separation: the
photon band is flat, the anharmonic parameter is of order one, and
universality protects nothing.  The numbers above are the spectroscopy
of the specific curl operator of the pyrochlore hexagons, in the
constrained spin-$\frac12$ gauge theory with no bare electric term ---
maximal lattice coupling, outside the reach of weak-coupling lattice
gauge theory.  The zone boundary is also not a corner case: it is where
the neutron-accessible momenta, the Monte Carlo damping~\cite{Huang2018},
and any finite-cluster momentum set live.  The lattice is not a
regulator here; it is the sample.

\emph{Implications.}---Three follow directly.  First, Gaussian fits of
$\Szz$ to neutron data --- the analysis underlying current photon
searches~\cite{Sibille2018,Gao2025} --- are least reliable exactly where
the accessible momenta lie: on the zone boundary, between a
sixth and two thirds of the electric-field spectral weight is not in
the one-photon line at all --- at $L$ the photon retains only a third
--- and the line itself is split by interactions.  Second, the
zone-boundary damping seen in quantum Monte Carlo~\cite{Huang2018}
acquires a concrete assignment: weight transferred to the
interaction-split photon doublet and the $C$-odd non-photon states.  Third, the
parity rule cleanly divides the labor between experiments: neutrons see
the one- and three-photon sectors; the two-photon continuum belongs
exclusively to bilinear probes (Raman, ultrasound, magnetostriction)
~\cite{Fu2017,Simon2022}, and coincidence of the bilinear-channel onset
at twice the neutron line would constitute a parameter-free
identification of the emergent photon pair.

\emph{Limitations.}---Results rest on two cluster sizes.  The roton
(energy, half-share of the weight, mechanism) and the multiparticle
fraction are stable from 48 to 64 sites; the $X$-point branch
splitting is not, and is reported descriptively only.  The one-loop
lineshape and the flux-resolved diagnostic are pending as marked.  The connection to
microscopic spin models is not addressed here: in a companion analysis of
the XXZ model with torus artifacts removed~\cite{ZhouKimMask} we find
$\sim\!10\%$ off-pole weight at $L$ at physical couplings, consistent
with the present mechanism, but there the photon-band states carry
appreciable virtual-spinon admixture and the comparison is deferred.

\emph{Conclusion.}---Treating the compactness of the emergent gauge
field exactly, rather than expanding it away, changes the dynamical
structure factor of quantum spin ice qualitatively.  Both photon
branches survive, split by their own interactions; at the zone
boundary one of them rotonizes --- dropping below the photon line at
the momentum where the ice liquid's equal-time correlations peak,
by the same Feynman--Bijl mechanism that produces the roton of
superfluid helium --- and becomes the lowest excitation of the theory,
carrying half of the neutron response; above both branches sits a
multiparticle continuum of up to a third of the spectral weight, with
an exact charge-conjugation rule routing the $C$-even sector to
non-magnetic probes.  The emergent
photon of quantum spin ice is not a free particle, and its interactions
are not a small correction --- they are large enough to be the next thing
experiments should look for.

